      "id": 1,
      "text": "Gowen was named for Francis I. Gowen, a Philadelphia attorney and president of the Choctaw, Oklahoma and Gulf Railroad Company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gowen%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Gowen originated as a small community in Indian Territory during the late 19th century within the lands of the Choctaw Nation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/gowen_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Gowen post office first opened its doors on January 13, 1894, during the Indian Territory era.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gowen%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Gowen emerged as a mining outpost in the late 19th century, contributing to the region's peak coal production by 1912.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/gowen_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Gowen is situated within the Ouachita Mountains region, characterized by rolling hills, folded ridges, and forested landscapes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/gowen_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The climate in Gowen is humid subtropical, featuring hot summers and mild winters influenced by moist air from the Gulf of Mexico.",
